     "text": "## Short answer\n\n**Negotiate. Don't accept as-is, and don't walk away yet.** The offer is reasonable, but it isn't strong enough to justify leaving a job you're happy in. You also have more leverage than most candidates: Google contacted you, you weren't looking, and staying at Meta is a good fallback.\n\n## How the offer compares\n\n| | Meta, next 12 months | Google, Year 1 (guaranteed) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Base | $240K | $235K |\n| Bonus | ~$36K | $47K (target) |\n| Equity vesting | ~$200K | $270K |\n| **Total** | **~$476K** | **~$552K** |\n\n- **Compared with your next 12 months at Meta:** about +$76K, or +16%.\n- **Compared with your usual ~$526K/yr:** about +$26K, or +5%. That's a small gain for the cost of switching jobs.\n- **The base is a pay cut.** $235K is below your current $240K and near the middle of the $183K\u2013$271K range. There's clear room to go higher.\n\n**The refresh illustration:** If Google's refreshes come through as the recruiter described, 4-year comp is about $2.58M, or roughly $645K/yr on average. Staying at Meta at ~$526K/yr comes to about $2.1M. Without any Google refreshes, the 4-year total is $2.21M. So:\n- Most of the upside depends on refreshes that aren't guaranteed. You'd also be earning them as a new hire who hasn't been through a performance review cycle yet.\n- After Year 4, Google settles at about $532K/yr ($235K + $47K + $250K), roughly the same as Meta today. The lasting advantage of moving comes from the **new-hire grant**, so that's where to push hardest.\n\n## What to ask for\n\n| Item | Ask | Realistic result |\n|---|---|---|\n| **New-hire RSUs** | **$1.4M** | $1.2\u20131.3M |\n| **Base** | **$265K** | $250\u2013260K |\n| **Sign-on bonus** | **$75K** | $40\u201360K |\n\nA realistic result would be about $250K base, $50K bonus, $1.25M in RSUs (about $312K/yr) and a $50K sign-on. That puts Year 1 around **$660K**, up from $552K, and 4-year guaranteed comp around **$2.5M** before any refreshes.\n\n**How to justify each ask:**\n- **Equity:** You'd be giving up unvested Meta RSUs: $200K in the next 12 months, and probably more beyond that. Add up your exact unvested total and use the number.\n- **Base:** Moving shouldn't mean a lower base. Your experience as a Meta IC6 supports a figure toward the top of their posted range.\n- **Sign-on:** This covers the bonus you'd lose by leaving Meta before it pays out, plus the vesting you'd give up in the first year. Expect a clawback if you leave within 12\u201324 months.\n\n**Suggested script:**\n> \"I'm genuinely excited about the role, but I'm happy at Meta and wasn't looking, so the move has to make clear financial sense. As structured, my base goes down and my first-year gain doesn't offset what I'm leaving behind. If you can get to $265K base, $1.4M in RSUs, and a $75K sign-on to cover the bonus and vesting I'd forfeit, I'm ready to sign.\"\n\nPut specific numbers on everything and tie the request to accepting. Recruiters can take a concrete package to the compensation team more easily than a vague \"can you do better?\"\n\n## Before you decide\n\n1. **Check the vesting schedule.** The recruiter's $270K in Year 1 means even vesting of 25% a year. Ask whether it's monthly or quarterly and whether there's a cliff. Get the schedule in writing.\n2. **Know your walk-away number before you negotiate.** Since you're happy at Meta, a sensible floor is about **$600K in guaranteed Year 1** and a base of at least $240K. If Google won't reach that, staying is a perfectly good choice.\n3. **Meta may counter.** Meta sometimes makes retention offers, but only raise it if you'd really be willing to leave.\n4. **Remember the non-pay costs.** You'd lose your track record and relationships, need time to get up to speed, and face the uncertainty of your first performance review, which affects those refreshes. That's why a 5% gain isn't enough. A gain of 20\u201325% or more in guaranteed comp is what makes this risk worth taking.\n\n**Bottom line:** As written, the offer is a modest raise, with most of the upside depending on refreshes that aren't guaranteed. Push hardest on the new-hire RSUs, get the base above your current $240K, and add a sign-on. If Google reaches about $640K\u2013660K in Year 1, accept. If they won't go above about $600K, staying at Meta is the better choice.",
