     "text": "## Short answer\n\n**Yes, I'd take it, once you've checked a few things below.** This offer clears the walk-away bar I suggested last time (at least $600K guaranteed in Year 1 and a base of at least $240K) by a wide margin. Don't push for more pay. They've said this is their best offer and they already gave you a lot. Use any remaining requests on the terms instead.\n\n## The numbers\n\n| | Meta (stay) | Google final offer (guaranteed) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Base | $240K | $240K |\n| Bonus (target) | ~$36K | $48K |\n| Equity vesting | ~$200K | $270K |\n| Sign-on | \u2013 | $200K |\n| **Year 1** | **~$476K** | **~$758K** |\n| **Years 2\u20134, per year** | ~$526K | ~$558K |\n| **4-year total** | **~$2.05M** | **~$2.43M** |\n\n- **Year 1:** about +$282K (+59%) compared with staying.\n- **4 years, guaranteed only:** about +$380K (+18%).\n- **If the recruiter's refresh example holds** (about $62.5K/yr added for each year's grant), Google's 4-year total is roughly **$2.8M**. Years 2\u20134 would be about $620K, $683K and $745K.\n- **Fair comparison:** Your Meta figures also assume Meta keeps giving you refreshes. Neither side's future equity is guaranteed, so comparing the two refresh scenarios is fair.\n\n## The catch\n\n**The gain is front-loaded.** Most of it is the sign-on bonus. After Year 1, the guaranteed gain drops to about +$32K/yr (+6%), so the long-term case depends on how you perform and get refreshed at Google. That risk was there before. The difference now is that the sign-on pays you well for taking it.\n\n## Before you sign\n\n1. **Clawback terms.** Find out whether you'd repay the sign-on in full or prorated, and for how long (usually 12\u201324 months). Ask for prorated repayment. Companies often agree to that when they won't move on pay.\n2. **When the sign-on is paid.** Check whether it comes in one lump sum or is split across Year 1 and Year 2. If it's split, Year 1 is lower than shown.\n3. **Tax on the sign-on.** It's usually withheld at the flat 22% federal supplemental rate, which is probably below your real marginal rate. Set money aside so you aren't hit with a bill at tax time.\n4. **Google's vesting schedule.** Get it in writing. The $270K/yr assumes even vesting. Some Google grants have been front-loaded, which would raise Year 1 and lower Years 3\u20134.\n5. **Time your start date around Meta.** Check your next RSU vest date and when your annual bonus pays out. Leaving just after a vest, or after the bonus pays, could keep tens of thousands that you'd otherwise lose. Asking Google for a start date that allows this is a normal request.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nOn guaranteed money, this beats staying by about $380K over 4 years. Most of that comes in Year 1, and the offer meets the bar you set. If the clawback terms are reasonable and the start date can work around your Meta vest, accept.\n\nThe remaining question isn't about pay. It's whether you're willing to give up a job you're happy in and the track record you've built there. That's for you to decide, but on compensation, this is a good offer.",
